  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The formula collapsed when it mattered most\u2014and Machado's power wasn't enough.",
    "what_this_means": "Arizona got ahead early and stayed there. The Padres chased from the second inning on, scratched out eleven hits, but couldn't finish. Machado kept them alive with two home runs and five RBIs\u2014he did his job. Michael King was sharp, holding them to just two earned runs over six innings. The numbers looked respectable. The result didn't. This is the fragility that haunts this team: they had the moments, just not the finish. King exited before the game was truly decided, and whatever came after didn't hold. A loss in Mexico City, a half-game from first place, and the inconsistency that's defined the season got louder.",
    "what_to_watch": "The pattern is becoming harder to ignore. This team has Machado driving runs when the game is on the line\u2014that's real, that's repeatable. But one player's power doesn't move you out of second place when the rest of the offense sits at .235. The pitching is holding up (3.96 ERA in a division that can hit). The problem is margin: they're winning on thin ones, and losing the same way. Machado's night proved the bats can show up. It didn't prove they can finish together. You need the whole thing to click in the same inning, not in relay. This loss wasn't about Arizona being better. It was about the Padres not being complete enough.",
